<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    A stripped-of-debug-info x86-64 Linux program calls poll, getrlimit and
    setrlimit. No built-in prototype table declares them, so the calls are
    untyped and a `struct pollfd` on the stack is three unrelated locals, one of
    them read without a write. P1 program prep, option typelib (default auto):
    the prebuilt type library declares the imports nothing else did, with the
    record layouts the platform's compiler measured.

    Pass 1 is `typelib off`, the old output (#1, #2). Pass 2 is the default: the
    pollfd is one object with its members (#3, #4, #5) and the rlimit copy is a
    member assignment (#6). Pass 3 reads a library file instead of the built-in
    one, and the members take that file's names (#7). `puts` is declared by the
    built-in table in every pass and is never the library's (#8).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
